# Local signing configuration for the dext and its installer app.
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your own values. .env is gitignored: the signing
# identity names a person, and the profiles are per-developer files tied to specific
# Macs. Nothing in here belongs in a public repository.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   make install
#
# This file is read by make, not by a shell, so values are taken LITERALLY:
#   - do not use quotes (they would become part of the value)
#   - $VAR is not expanded
#   - a leading ~/ is expanded by the Makefile as a convenience, but nothing else is
#
# Leave everything empty or delete .env to get an ad-hoc signed build. That only loads
# with the AMFI boot-arg, which is a machine-wide security regression and not the
# supported path -- see README.md.

# The exact identity name from: security find-identity -v -p codesigning
# Include the parenthesised suffix.
SIGN_ID=Apple Development: you@example.com (XXXXXXXXXX)

# Provisioning profiles downloaded from developer.apple.com. Both must list this Mac's
# Provisioning UDID, which is NOT its hardware UUID or serial:
#   system_profiler SPHardwareDataType | grep "Provisioning UDID"
#
# The dext needs a DriverKit App Development profile with the DriverKit, DriverKit Family
# Networking and DriverKit USB Transport capabilities. The installer needs a macOS App
# Development profile with System Extension. Check what a profile actually grants with
# tools/inspect-profile/inspect-profile.sh.
DEXT_PROFILE=~/Downloads/YourDriver_DriverKit_Development.provisionprofile
APP_PROFILE=~/Downloads/YourInstaller_macOS_Development.provisionprofile

# Your Apple Team ID. Normally leave this unset: the Makefile reads it out of the
# profile above, which guarantees it matches the profile it has to agree with. Set it
# only if you are signing without a profile.
#TEAM_ID=
